{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795291884,"nonce":"n8Eain2s5UlPHY8kcI1FKlM858w=","title":"The loop safepoint poll stops LLVM vectorizing call-free kernels: SoA loops run 2 to 4 times slower than hxcpp","message":"Reported by a peer project (hxn): a struct-of-arrays physics update over 4096 Singles is about 2.6 times slower on Ash than on hxcpp. Their guess was a bounds check or a field reload stopping vectorization. The cause is the safepoint poll in the loop. hl.NativeArray itself is not the problem.\n\nKernel (their Bench.hx, 4000 repetitions per timed round, best of 6 after the first):\n\n    for (i in 0...4096) { w.vel[i] = w.vel[i] + g*dt; w.pos[i] = w.pos[i] + w.vel[i]*dt; }\n\nwith `vel` and `pos` as object fields holding hl.NativeArray\u003cSingle\u003e, hl.Bytes, or a Float32Buffer wrapper over hl.Bytes. Source and bytecode: bufbench/Bench.hx and bench.hl in the hxn session's scratchpad (/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-amaterasu-Vibranium-hxn/ef59bc9e-c7eb-45dd-a284-950fd71c47ea/scratchpad/bufbench); a copy is in this session's scratchpad under buf/.\n\nns per element, Mac, v0.1.21 plus local commits, load average 6 to 14:\n\n                          NativeArray   hl.Bytes   Bytes+16B header   Float32Buffer\n    default hybrid           0.59         0.56         0.59              0.58\n    llvm, polls on           0.58         0.56         0.59              0.58\n    llvm, ASH_FIBER_POLLS=0  0.26         0.13         0.13              0.14\n    hybrid, polls off        0.22         0.10         0.10              0.11\n    hxcpp unsafeGet/Set      0.22   (plain Array\u003cSingle\u003e indexing, with bounds checks: 2.23)\n\nEvidence from ASH_DUMP_FN_IR (post middle end, what executes):\n- Polls on: one scalar loop. Per iteration the two array pointers are reloaded from the object, then `and i64 %iv.next, 255` tests whether a poll is due; the poll block holds a volatile load, an atomic load and an indirect call. LLVM's loop vectorizer does not vectorize a loop with a call and volatile or atomic accesses on a path, and the call also pins the field reloads.\n- Polls off: the same loop becomes `\u003c4 x float\u003e` unrolled twice (8 elements per trip) behind one runtime overlap check of the two arrays. Nothing else changed.\n\nSo the in-loop poll costs 2 to 5 times on call-free kernels, which are the data-oriented loops that game physics, particles and animation are made of. 9d82d77 chose a test in front of the poll over a real strip loop because the loop split made the allocators spill loop-carried values on a call benchmark. That choice leaves the call inside the loop.\n\nSecond effect, smaller. hl.NativeArray data starts 24 bytes into the object (HL varray header), so its 16-byte vector accesses are 8 bytes off a 16-byte boundary. A hl.Bytes wrapper with a 24-byte header, otherwise the same code, runs 0.223 against 0.135 with a 16-byte header (polls off). hl.NativeArray is 0.257, close to the misaligned one. The ABI fixes the header, so this is only worth documenting, or loop peeling for alignment if LLVM can be made to do it.\n\nOptions for the poll:\n- LLVM tier only: for an innermost loop with no calls and an induction variable, emit a real nested loop. The outer loop polls once per strip, the inner loop has no call and vectorizes. AIR stays as it is, so the interpreter, OSR transfer and the Cranelift allocation are untouched. The spill cost that sank the AIR-level split was measured on call benchmarks; call-free loops carry few values. Not measured.\n- Skip the poll for a call-free loop with a constant trip count up to a bound, so latency stays bounded. Covers fixed-size kernels like this one, not loops over a runtime length.\n- Do both: the second where it applies, the first elsewhere.\n\nRelated: 9d82d77 (strip-mine as a test, closed), 8993f81 (strip-mined loops that call natives, closed).\n\nOpen:\n- Confirmed on the Mac only so far; a NUC (x86) run of the same switch is queued.\n- Whether Ash's own AIR widener fires on `for (i in 0...N)` was not checked.\n- Not checked whether game-shaped code has the same loops with calls inside, where this does not help.","files":null}]}